\name{PheatmapTreeLayerProcessor}
\alias{PheatmapTreeLayerProcessor}
\title{pheatmap Dendrogram Layer Processor}
\description{
Reads one of a pheatmap's dendrograms as a \code{tree} layer, from the
\code{hclust} it was drawn from (\code{tree_row} or \code{tree_col}); see
\code{\link[=pheatmap_tree_nodes]{pheatmap_tree_nodes()}} for the nodes. Experimental.

A side has a tree only when it was clustered and the tree is drawn: not
with \code{cluster_rows = FALSE}, nor with \code{treeheight_row = 0}, which orders
the rows by the tree but draws none. With \code{kmeans_k} the row tree is the
one pheatmap draws, over the cluster centres. \code{cutree_rows} only spaces
the drawing; the tree is unchanged.

Each leaf is named as the heat layer names its row or column
(\code{\link[=pheatmap_axis_labels]{pheatmap_axis_labels()}}), in the same order.

\strong{Selectors.} One per node, in node order: the unseen mark
\code{\link[=pheatmap_mark_tree]{pheatmap_mark_tree()}} drew at it, which the orchestrator adds before the
layer is read. pheatmap draws a dendrogram as one polyline per merge --
\code{n - 1} marks for \verb{2n - 1} nodes, nothing at a leaf -- so the marks are
what the highlight lands on. When they could not be placed on the drawn
links there are none, and no selectors: a highlight that cannot be
aligned is left out rather than placed on the wrong link.
}
